====================================================================
DOMAIN: Historical Development, Teleology, and the Origins of Historicism
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-09
PAPERS_FOUND: 14 total (High: 8, Medium: 5, Low: 1)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The secondary literature on Herder's philosophy of history organizes itself around
three interlocking debates. The first concerns Herder's relationship to historicism:
following Friedrich Meinecke's influential thesis in Die Entstehung des Historismus
(1936), scholars long credited Herder with founding the historicist tradition through
his insistence on individuality, organic development, and Einfuehlung. This reading
has been complicated by Beiser (2011), Iggers (1968/1983), and Zammito (2009), who
show that the "father of historicism" attribution involved selective reading -- Herder's
emphases on providence, natural law, and Humanitaet sit uneasily with the later
positivist Rankean tradition that claimed him.

The second debate concerns Herder's teleology: whether his philosophy of history
posits a single overarching telos (Humanitaet as the goal of all history) or is
irreducibly pluralist, granting each people and period autonomous worth. Wells (1960)
identified an apparent contradiction between the "relativist" Auch eine Philosophie
(1774) and the more developmental Ideen (1784-91). Subsequent scholarship (Bollacher
2017, James 2022, Bristow 2025) has sought to dissolve or theorize this tension: Herder
embraces both the intrinsic worth of each historical stage and a weak universal telos
that does not subordinate any period to another.

The third debate concerns the explanatory role of Kraft (force) and organic metaphors
in Herder's genetic method. Nassar (2015), Hampton (2015), and Waldow (2017) trace how
Herder's borrowing from natural history -- epigenesis, analogical reasoning, organic
totality -- shapes his account of how cultures develop through internal forces rather
than external causes, yielding a model of historical explanation that rivals both
Kantian mechanism and Enlightenment stadial theory.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain is central to the project: it directly addresses how Herder models
historical development (organic growth, Kraft, genetic explanation), his contested
role as founder of historicism, the structure of his teleology in the Ideen, and the
tension between providential universalism and the autonomous worth of each age -- the
core interpretive problems of his philosophy of history.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Meinecke's Die Entstehung des Historismus (1936) is the unavoidable point of reference
for the historicism debate but has no DOI and the German text is only partially
translated; the secondary literature discusses it extensively but direct engagement
with the text is mediated. The reception of Herder's Ideen in natural science
contexts (Buffon, Blumenbach, epigenesis) is covered patchily in the philosophical
literature.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The Wells (1960) thesis about "two philosophies" provides a useful structuring frame:
the synthesis should work through whether more recent scholarship (Bollacher, James,
Bristow) genuinely resolves or merely displaces the tension between relativism and
teleological universalism. The Beiser chapter gives the most systematic account of
Herder's historicism and should anchor the historicism section.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Herder as founder/precursor of historicism (Meinecke, Iggers, Beiser): 4 papers
- Organic development and Kraft as explanatory framework (Nassar, Hampton, Waldow): 3 papers
- Teleology and Humanitaet (Bollacher, James, Wells, Bristow): 4 papers
- Hermeneutics and Einfuehlung (Gjesdal, Mertel, Zammito): 3 papers
====================================================================
}

@book{beiser2011german,
  author    = {Beiser, Frederick C.},
  title     = {The German Historicist Tradition},
  year      = {2011},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  doi       = {10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199691555.001.0001},
  abstract  = {This is the first full study in English of the German historicist tradition. Frederick C. Beiser surveys the major German thinkers on history from the middle of the eighteenth century until the early twentieth century, providing an introduction to each thinker and the main issues in interpreting and appraising his thought. The volume offers new interpretations of well-known philosophers such as Johann Gottfried Herder and Max Weber, and introduces others who are scarcely known at all.},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Offers the first systematic English-language account of German historicism from Herder through Weber, arguing that historicism constitutes a coherent philosophical tradition defined by commitments to individuality, development, and the irreducibility of human sciences to natural science. The chapter on Herder (pp. 98-166) treats his historicism as emerging from a critique of Enlightenment rationalism and natural law theory, while remaining embedded in a theology of Providence.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most thorough reconstruction in English of the intellectual origins of Herder's historicist commitments and their relation to later developments; an indispensable reference for situating both the Auch eine Philosophie and the Ideen in the historicist tradition.

  POSITION: Broadly supportive of Meinecke's attribution of historicism's origins to Herder, while complicating it by foregrounding the theological and naturalist dimensions that the later Rankean tradition suppressed.
  },
  keywords  = {historicism, Herder, German-idealism, teleology, High}
}

@incollection{beiser2011herder,
  author    = {Beiser, Frederick C.},
  title     = {Herder's Historicism, its Genesis and Development},
  booktitle = {The German Historicist Tradition},
  year      = {2011},
  pages     = {98--166},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  doi       = {10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199691555.003.0004},
  abstract  = {Chapter treating Herder as the originator of the historicist tradition, tracing his historicism through the Auch eine Philosophie and Ideen. Beiser argues that Herder's genetic method, his insistence on cultural individuality, and his naturalization of reason within history constitute the core contributions to historicism, even as his providential teleology complicates any straightforwardly secular reading.},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reconstructs the genesis of Herder's historicism from his early confrontation with Enlightenment universalism through the mature Ideen, identifying three key commitments -- individuality (each culture has its own standard), development (cultures grow organically), and immanence (no appeal to transcendent norms) -- that define the historicist programme. Traces how Herder's naturalism and providential theology are kept in tension rather than resolved.

  RELEVANCE: The most analytically precise account of what makes Herder a historicist in the philosophical sense; directly relevant to the question of whether and how his teleology (Humanitaet) is compatible with cultural relativism.

  POSITION: Argues Herder is the founding figure of historicism proper, not merely a precursor, while acknowledging the tensions in his thought that later thinkers would variously resolve.
  },
  keywords  = {historicism, Herder, Auch-eine-Philosophie, Ideen, genetic-explanation, High}
}

@article{wells1960herder,
  author    = {Wells, G. A.},
  title     = {Herder's Two Philosophies of History},
  journal   = {Journal of the History of Ideas},
  year      = {1960},
  volume    = {21},
  number    = {4},
  pages     = {527--537},
  doi       = {10.2307/2708100},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Identifies a fundamental tension between Herder's two major historical works: the Auch eine Philosophie (1774) is presented as relativist and anti-teleological, celebrating each epoch's intrinsic worth and rejecting Enlightenment progressivism, while the Ideen (1784-91) advances a developmental teleology with Humanitaet as the directional goal of all history. Wells argues these represent genuinely different philosophical commitments rather than a single coherent position.

  RELEVANCE: Sets the terms for subsequent debate about whether Herder held a coherent philosophy of history; the "two philosophies" thesis remains the orienting problem for much subsequent scholarship (Bollacher, James, Bristow) and is directly relevant to how the project characterizes Herder's teleology.

  POSITION: Critical reading that stresses inconsistency rather than tension-within-unity; subsequent scholarship has largely tried to refute or modulate this verdict.
  },
  keywords  = {Auch-eine-Philosophie, Ideen, teleology, relativism, historicism, High},
  abstract = {Both Stadelmann and Meinecke consider Herder's early philosophy of history, the Auch eine Philosophie der Geschichte, written in Buckeburg in 1774, to be a greater achievement than the Ideen zur Philosophie der Geschichte der Menschheit of his Weimar period, published in four volumes between 1784 and 1791. I propose in this paper to examine the reasons they give for their preference. My justification for doing so is, first, that they are regarded as important authorities on Herder, and second, that their methods of evaluating his typify an approach to history and historians common today. Stadelmann 'considers that since Herder holds all historical phenomena to be determined by their antecedents, he is not entitled to criticize the medieval clergy and aristocracy for their behavior; but that this is what he does in the Ideen, whereas in the early Auch eine Philosophie he was much more detached and felt his way into the past instead of passing judgment on it. In the Ideen, he says, Herder's intuitive genial [kongenial] understanding for the religious and aristocratic greatness of the Middle Ages is killed by strong liberal-republican and Protestant antipathy to the aristocratic constitution that suppressed the industry and inventiveness of the artisans, and to the tyranny the clergy exercised over men's minds. He holds that the Auch eine Philosophie is free from this defect and that Herder begins to lose his detachment only after his move to Weimar.1 This is an extraordinary statement, especially as Stadelmann himself admits that the Auch eine Philosophie is a virulent attack on modern decadence-an attack effected by boosting the medieval past. Herder, he says,  plays off the Middle Ages against modern times as heart against head, achievement against morbid reflection, firm-rootedness against dissipation  (23). If the criticism of the medieval world in the Ideen constitutes a defect in that work, then the criticism of the present in the Auch eine Philosophie is a like blemish, and later in his book Stadelmann admits that this is so:  In the Biickeburg paper Herder abandons his universal-historical standpoint when he comes to speak of the present  (89). So the criterion of detachment is, after all, useless as a means of evaluating the two works, and Stadelmann sets to work to find another: for of the fact he has no doubt:  The Humanititsbriefe and even the Ideen are further removed from true historical sense than Herder's youthful works (57). The following passage is an attempt to justify this view:},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{mertel2014historicism,
  author    = {Mertel, Kurt C. M.},
  title     = {Historicism and Critique in {H}erder's \textit{Another Philosophy of History}: Some Hermeneutic Reflections},
  journal   = {European Journal of Philosophy},
  year      = {2014},
  volume    = {24},
  number    = {2},
  pages     = {397--416},
  doi       = {10.1111/ejop.12088},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Herder's stated project in the Auch eine Philosophie -- merely describing each culture without comparative judgment -- is undermined by a tension between his doctrine of historicism and cultural relativism on the one hand, and his actual practice of critique on the other. Drawing on Gadamer's philosophical hermeneutics, Mertel shows that understanding presupposes justification and that prejudice is enabling rather than merely distorting, which discloses two conditions of possibility for Herder's project of which Herder himself was unaware.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the Auch eine Philosophie and the question of whether historicism and critique are compatible in Herder; relevant to how the project frames Herder's methodology and the tensions in his historical relativism.

  POSITION: Gadamerian reading that reconstructs Herder's practice as more coherent than his stated doctrine, using hermeneutic philosophy to resolve what appeared as contradiction.
  },
  keywords  = {Auch-eine-Philosophie, historicism, hermeneutics, Gadamer, relativism, High},
  abstract = {Abstract In Another Philosophy of History , J . G . H erder claims that his aim is not to compare and judge different cultures, but merely to describe and explain how each came into being and thus to adopt the standpoint of an impartial observer. I argue, however, that there is a tension between H erder's understanding of his own project—his stated doctrine of historicism and cultural relativism—and the way in which it is actually put into practice. That is, despite H erder's stated aims, he is nevertheless unable to avoid justifying premodern forms of life and making context‐transcending evaluative judgments in the process of trying to understand cultures on their own terms and holding them up as exemplars vis à vis the Enlightenment. This tension presents the challenge of accounting for it in the most charitable and illuminating way. I argue that this goal can be achieved by appealing to the resources of the philosophical hermeneutics of H ans‐ G eorg G adamer, which enables us to disclose two enabling conditions for H erder's project of which he was not explicitly aware, viz., the internal connection between understanding and justification and the enabling character of prejudice as the condition for the possibility of understanding as such.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{james2022situating,
  author    = {James, David},
  title     = {Situating the {E}nlightenment in {H}erder's Philosophy of History},
  journal   = {Journal of the Philosophy of History},
  year      = {2022},
  volume    = {16},
  number    = {3},
  pages     = {247--270},
  doi       = {10.1163/18722636-12341480},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that abstract thinking and hostility to prejudice -- features Herder famously criticizes in Enlightenment thought -- are themselves integral to Herder's project of situating the Enlightenment within his philosophy of history. The Enlightenment makes a distinctive contribution to humanity's development, but this cannot make it superior to prior ages, since each age has equal value as a contribution to the full development of Humanitaet.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the core tension between Herder's critique of Enlightenment progressivism and his own commitment to a teleological conception of Humanitaet; provides a careful recent reconstruction of how Herder reconciles the intrinsic worth of each period with a weak universal telos.

  POSITION: Defends a coherentist reading against Wells's two-philosophies thesis; Herder's universalism and his particularism are mutually constraining rather than contradictory.
  },
  keywords  = {Humanitaet, teleology, Enlightenment, Ideen, pluralism, High},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
 Although Herder is critical of the Enlightenment, I show that his philosophy of history commits him to the claim that the age and culture shaped by the Enlightenment in some way makes a distinctive contribution to the development of humanity. Yet this contribution cannot make this age and culture superior to earlier ones, for this would violate Herder’s commitment to the principle that each age and culture ought to be accorded an equal status because of the equal value of its contribution to the full development of humanity. I argue that an abstract way of thinking and a hostility to prejudice are integral elements of Herder’s attempt to situate the Enlightenment in his philosophy of history, despite how he otherwise criticizes the Enlightenment for its abstract thinking and its hostility to prejudice.},
}

@article{bristow2025herder,
  author    = {Bristow, William},
  title     = {Herder, Philosophy and Universal History},
  journal   = {The European Legacy},
  year      = {2025},
  volume    = {30},
  number    = {6},
  pages     = {712--727},
  doi       = {10.1080/10848770.2025.2525597},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines the philosophical status of Herder's claim that history has a universal direction toward Humanitaet, arguing that this universalism is philosophically grounded and not merely rhetorical, but that it is structured so as to be compatible with the irreducible individuality and worth of particular peoples and epochs.

  RELEVANCE: The most recent sustained treatment of Herder's philosophy of universal history; directly engages the project's question of whether Humanitaet is a single goal or a regulative idea compatible with plural ends.

  POSITION: Moderate universalist reading that seeks to vindicate Herder's teleological claims without sacrificing his commitment to cultural particularity.
  },
  keywords  = {Humanitaet, universal-history, teleology, pluralism, High},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT In this article I propose that the tension between two of Herder’s most important contributions to Western thought and culture, the incomparability thesis and the history thesis, is reconciled by Herder’s own innovative project and method of universal history. Whereas the incomparability thesis demands that peoples are known in their uniqueness without comparison to other peoples, the history thesis implies knowing peoples in relation to each other as distinct parts of a universal history of humanity. I interpret Herder’s method and project of universal history as a precursor of Hegel’s project and method of his Phenomenology of Spirit. The comparison between Herder’s and Hegel’s methods and projects shows that Herder’s universal history is a philosophical inquiry—empirical, critical, skeptical, and open-ended—into the meaning and telos of human existence. I propose in concluding that Herder’s innovative practice of philosophy as universal history is a third significant contribution to European thought.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{gjesdal2017herder,
  author    = {Gjesdal, Kristin},
  title     = {Herder's Hermeneutics: History, Poetry, Enlightenment},
  year      = {2017},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi       = {10.1017/9781316285770},
  abstract  = {Herder's Hermeneutics offers a comprehensive study of Herder's theory of understanding as applied to history, poetry, and the Enlightenment. Gjesdal argues that Herder develops a non-essentialist approach to art, history, and reason that constitutes a systematic alternative to both Hegel's essentialist historicism and later Romantic subjectivism.},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Herder develops a philosophically coherent hermeneutics grounded in historical situatedness, empathetic understanding (Einfuehlung), and attention to poetic form that anticipates later nineteenth-century hermeneutics but is irreducible to it. Reconstructs how Herder's historical method is shaped by his engagement with biblical criticism, classical philology, and Enlightenment debates about taste.

  RELEVANCE: Most thorough recent account of the hermeneutic and methodological foundations of Herder's historical thinking; directly relevant to the question of what Herder means by understanding a historical period "from within" and how this relates to his genetic method.

  POSITION: Positions Herder as offering a non-essentialist alternative to Hegel that merits rehabilitation on its own terms rather than as a precursor to later thought.
  },
  keywords  = {hermeneutics, Einfuehlung, historical-method, Ideen, Enlightenment, High}
}

@book{sikka2011herder,
  author    = {Sikka, Sonia},
  title     = {Herder on Humanity and Cultural Difference: Enlightened Relativism},
  year      = {2011},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi       = {10.1017/CBO9780511783012},
  abstract  = {Examines Herder's cultural relativism in its philosophical context, arguing that Herder's relativism is consistent with a commitment to Humanitaet as a universal value. Analyses the relationship between cultural difference, human nature, and historical development across Herder's major works.},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Herder's relativism is not inconsistent with his commitment to Humanitaet: the latter functions as a thin universal value that each culture realizes differently rather than as a substantive norm against which cultures are measured. Cultural difference is positively valued as the vehicle through which humanity expresses its full range of capacities.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the tension between pluralism and universalism that is central to the project; argues for a coherent reading of Herder's teleology that respects both poles.

  POSITION: Defends "enlightened relativism" as Herder's considered view: relativism about evaluative standards combined with universalism about human nature and dignity.
  },
  keywords  = {Humanitaet, relativism, cultural-difference, teleology, High}
}

@book{barnard2003herder,
  author    = {Barnard, F.M.},
  title     = {Herder on Nationality, Humanity, and History},
  year      = {2003},
  publisher = {McGill-Queen's University Press},
  doi       = {10.1515/9780773570917},
  abstract  = {Synthesizes Barnard's decades of work on Herder's political and historical thought, examining the relationships among nationality, humanity, and historical development. Argues that Herder's philosophy of history is structured around the tension between the particularity of national communities and the universality of human nature.},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Synthesizes Barnard's extensive work on Herder to argue that nationality and humanity are not competing values but complementary poles in Herder's thought: particular nations are the organic units through which universal human capacities are developed and expressed. History is the process through which this double articulation unfolds.

  RELEVANCE: Barnard is the most important Anglophone scholar of Herder's political and historical thought; this volume provides the fullest account of how Herder reconciles nationalism, cultural particularity, and universal humanity -- the very tension the project needs to address.

  POSITION: Reconciliationist reading that treats Herder as holding a coherent philosophy in which organic nationality is the necessary vehicle of Humanitaet.
  },
  keywords  = {Humanitaet, nationality, organic-development, Ideen, historicism, High}
}

@article{nassar2015analogy,
  author    = {Nassar, Dalia},
  title     = {Analogy, Natural History and the Philosophy of Nature: {K}ant, {H}erder and the Problem of Empirical Science},
  journal   = {Journal of the Philosophy of History},
  year      = {2015},
  volume    = {9},
  number    = {2},
  pages     = {240--257},
  doi       = {10.1163/18722636-12341302},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Traces Kant's series of critical reviews of Herder's Ideen (1785) to show that the Kant-Herder dispute is fundamentally about the legitimacy of analogical reasoning in natural history: Herder uses analogy to bridge the gap between empirical observation and philosophical explanation of nature's purposes, while Kant insists that such speculative analogies exceed the limits of legitimate empirical science.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates the philosophical foundations of Herder's genetic-analogical method -- the use of natural history models (organic growth, epigenesis, the great chain of being) in his philosophy of history -- and the Kantian critique that challenged this methodology; directly relevant to the role of Kraft and organic metaphors in Herder's explanatory framework.

  POSITION: Positions Herder's analogical method as a philosophically serious alternative to Kantian critique, not merely a confused proto-scientific speculation.
  },
  keywords  = {Kant-Herder, analogy, natural-history, Kraft, organic-development, genetic-explanation, Medium},
  abstract = {In 1785 Kant published a series of critical reviews of Johann Gottfried Herder’s Ideas for a Philosophy of the History of Humanity (1784–1785), in which he not only challenges Herder’s conception of nature but also, and more importantly, his methodology. Kant’s complaint is that by relying on analogy, Herder draws deeply mistaken conclusions that overlook fundamental differences between human and nonhuman beings. But was Kant’s critique of Herder entirely fair? And how does it compare to Kant’s own use of analogy? My claim is that Herder’s use of analogy posed a fundamental methodological challenge to Kant, a challenge he sought to meet in the years following the reviews. In so doing, however, Kant found himself in the untenable situation of, on the one hand, granting analogy greater significance, and, on the other, severely restricting its use. By tracing the shifts in Kant’s thought through the lens of analogy, I aim to show that Kant’s transformed understanding of analogy reveals a fundamental tension between his a priori “metaphysics of nature” and empirical science, a tension that fundamentally shaped the philosophies of nature after Kant.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{hampton2015kraft,
  author    = {Hampton, Alexander J. B.},
  title     = {An {E}nglish Source of {G}erman {R}omanticism: {H}erder's {C}udworth Inspired Revision of {S}pinoza from `{P}lastik' to `{K}raft'},
  journal   = {The Heythrop Journal},
  year      = {2015},
  volume    = {58},
  number    = {3},
  pages     = {417--431},
  doi       = {10.1111/heyj.12272},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Herder's key concept of Kraft (force) as a middle principle between mechanism and teleology is shaped not only by Leibniz and Spinoza but by the Cambridge Platonist Ralph Cudworth, whose Plastic Nature provided a model for an immanent organizing principle that is neither mechanistic nor crudely teleological. This English source has been largely overlooked in the German-idealism literature.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the philosophical genealogy of Herder's central explanatory concept, Kraft; relevant to understanding what kind of explanatory work Kraft does in Herder's genetic account of historical development and how it differs from Kant's teleology.

  POSITION: Argues for a revisionist intellectual history of Kraft that introduces a neo-Platonic strand into what is usually read as a purely German debate with Spinoza and Leibniz.
  },
  keywords  = {Kraft, Spinoza, Cudworth, organic-development, teleology, Medium},
  abstract = {This examination considers the influence of the seventeenth century Cambridge Platonist Cudworth upon the thought of the late eighteenth century German thinker Herder. It focuses upon Herder's use of Cudworth's philosophy to create a revised version of Spinoza's metaphysics. Both Cudworth and Herder were concerned with the problem of determinism. Cudworth outlined a number of difficulties relating to this problem in the thought of Spinoza and proposed amendments, particularly the introduction of the middle principle of plastik , which would mediate between the Ideas of transcendent reason and mechanical materialism. We find these amendments to Spinoza's philosophy also employed in Herder's contribution to the Pantheism Controversy, in which he too offers a revised Spinozism and introduces his own middle principle of Kraft . This demonstrates an important but under‐explored English contribution to a key development in German intellectual history. The Pantheism Controversy was an epoch‐making event, helping to bring an end to the German Enlightenment and to inaugurate the Romantic movement. Herder's version of Spinoza's thought revived the philosopher's fortunes, and Herder's notion of Kraft became central to Romantic aesthetics. Finally, Herder's use of Cudworth demonstrates the important but overlooked source of Platonic realism in German Romantic thought.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{zammito2009metanarrative,
  author    = {Zammito, John},
  title     = {Herder and Historical Metanarrative: What's Philosophical about History?},
  booktitle = {A Companion to the Works of Johann Gottfried Herder},
  year      = {2009},
  pages     = {65--92},
  publisher = {Boydell and Brewer Limited},
  doi       = {10.1017/9781571137289.004},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that the "father of historicism" label, standardly derived from Meinecke and Stadelmann, involves retrospective redemption: Herder was absorbed into the historicist tradition of Ranke and Droysen by being selectively read. Zammito shows that what Herder himself was doing philosophically -- offering a metanarrative about the purpose and direction of history -- differs substantially from the later positivist historicism that claimed him as founder.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the Meinecke thesis and its problems; provides the most critical account of the "father of historicism" attribution, showing that Herder's providential teleology and metanarrative ambitions are foreign to the disciplinary matrix of nineteenth-century historicism.

  POSITION: Revisionist: challenges the received narrative by showing the discontinuity between Herder's philosophical ambitions and the later historicist tradition.
  },
  keywords  = {historicism, Meinecke, metanarrative, Ranke, providence, Medium},
  abstract = {THERE IS SOME TRUTH in Goethe's pronouncement that Johann Gottfried Herder's ideas had been both absorbed and forgotten by the conventional wisdom of German culture after 1800. He was absorbed either into the project of the Jena Romantiker and their literary hermeneutics or into the project of Hegel and his philosophy of history. But after the rise of historicism in the school of Ranke and Droysen, a retrospective redemption seemed possible. Thus, in the classic formulations of Meinecke and Stadelmann, Herder was resurrected as the "father of historicism." He was credited with pioneering the stress on individuality, development, and the "historical sense" of Einfühlung (or Verstehen [empathy]) that became the core of the disciplinary matrix of history in the nineteenth century.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{bollacher2017individualism,
  author    = {Bollacher, Martin},
  title     = {Individualism and Universalism in {H}erder's Conception of the Philosophy of History},
  booktitle = {Herder: Philosophy and Anthropology},
  editor    = {Waldow, Anik and DeSouza, Nigel},
  year      = {2017},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  doi       = {10.1093/oso/9780198779650.003.0012},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Uses Herder's "globe ontology" -- the spherical earth as a matrix structuring the contrast between particular peoples and universal humanity -- to argue that Herder's philosophy of history is organized around a productive opposition rather than a contradiction. The individuality of cultures and the universality of human species are mutually constitutive: Humanitaet is realized only through the full diversity of particular peoples, not despite it.

  RELEVANCE: Directly engages the tension between universal telos and particular worth in Herder's Ideen; the globe metaphor provides a concrete image for how Herder holds plurality and universality together without reducing either.

  POSITION: Coherentist reading that frames the individuality-universality tension as a structural feature of Herder's thought rather than an inconsistency to be overcome.
  },
  keywords  = {Humanitaet, universalism, individualism, Ideen, teleology, Medium},
  abstract = {Herder was very familiar with the concept of human rights (in his German, “Menschenrechte”), strongly shared the moral ideals of protecting people at which the concept aims, but tended to avoid using the concept himself. Why this ambivalence? The present article argues that a number of well-founded concerns about the concept underlie, or at least may underlie, his reservation, including concerns about the <italic>legal</italic> rather than <italic>moral</italic> nature of the very concept of “rights,” the undue restriction of the focus of “human rights” to protecting people against threats that emanate from their own governments rather than against those from outside (imperialism, colonialism, etc.) as well, and the concept’s dubious restriction of protections to human beings to the exclusion of animals. The article also argues, however, that these concerns are not insurmountable, and that indeed Herder himself has provided some of the most important theoretical tools for surmounting them.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{iggers1968german,
  author    = {Iggers, Georg G.},
  title     = {The German Conception of History: The National Tradition of Historical Thought from Herder to the Present},
  year      = {1968},
  publisher = {Wesleyan University Press},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Provides the first comprehensive critical examination in English of the German national tradition of historiography from Herder to Meinecke and beyond. Iggers argues that the tradition rests on the Lutheran-historicist thesis that existing institutions have rational grounding in God's will while all values must be understood within historical flux; this led to a distinctively German accommodation of liberal constitutional ideals to authoritarian state structures.

  RELEVANCE: Essential for understanding how Herder's cosmopolitan, culture-oriented nationalism was transformed into the state-centered nationalism of later historicism; contextualizes both the Meinecke thesis and the limits of reading Herder as the simple founder of the tradition.

  POSITION: Critical of the conservative political consequences of historicism; treats Herder's contribution as ambiguous -- his cultural pluralism points away from the statist nationalism that claimed him.
  },
